 # Trajectory Arrow Rendering
 
-Also called: speed lines, particle trajectory visualization
+Also called: speed lines
 
 ## Overview
-Trajectory Arrow Rendering is an illustrative technique that overlays directional arrows on volume renderings of time-varying particle systems to visualize feature movement and provide temporal context within a single static image [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sequential volume renderings, which make it difficult to track features visually, this method integrates pathline geometry into the volumetric depth buffer to highlight flow properties such as separation, convergence, and recirculation [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard volume rendering by explicitly decoupling the representation of particle trajectories from the scalar field density, using a multi-pass approach to ensure arrows are correctly occluded by and blended with the volume interior [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Flow and Trajectory Visualization](20165-flow-and-trajectory-visualization.md) and differs from sibling methods like [Flow Volumes](19010-flow-volumes.md) by rendering explicit trajectory geometry rather than volumetric flow representations.
+Trajectory Arrow Rendering is an illustrative technique that overlays directional arrows on volume renderings of time-varying particle systems to visualize feature movement and provide temporal context within a single static image [S1, unofficial]. Unlike viewing sequential volume renderings, which makes it difficult to track features visually, this method integrates pathline geometry into the volumetric depth buffer to highlight flow properties such as separation, convergence, and recirculation [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard volume rendering by explicitly decoupling the representation of particle trajectories from the scalar field density, using a multi-pass approach to ensure arrows are correctly occluded by and blended with the volume interior [S1, unofficial].
 
